B.OM=s(["Triggered by an unexpected startle","Often associated with an underlying structural brain abnormality or perinatal injury","Asymmetric tonic posturing is typical"],t.s)
B.WV=new A.cd("Startle epilepsy","Reflex epilepsy","Seizures triggered by a sudden, unexpected stimulus (loud noise, tap), typically producing an asymmetric tonic seizure.",B.OM,"Rapid generalized or bilateral discharge time-locked to the startle stimulus.",null,!1)
B.O1=s(["Onset typically 3-12 months of age","Clustered brief spasms (flexion, extension, or mixed)","Developmental regression is a red flag","Requires urgent evaluation \u2014 ACTH/vigabatrin are first-line"],t.s)
B.WW=new A.cd("Infantile spasms (West syndrome)","Epilepsy syndrome","Clusters of brief flexor, extensor, or mixed spasms in infancy, part of West syndrome (spasms, hypsarrhythmia, developmental regression).",B.O1,"Hypsarrhythmia: chaotic, high-amplitude slow activity with multifocal spikes.","Infantile spasms are a pediatric neurologic emergency \u2014 early treatment improves developmental outcome.",!1)
B.Mz=s(["Early-morning myoclonic jerks, often shortly after awakening","Generalized tonic-clonic seizures are common","Sleep deprivation and alcohol are classic triggers","Lifelong treatment is usually required \u2014 high relapse rate off medication"],t.s)
B.Xd=new A.cd("Juvenile myoclonic epilepsy","Epilepsy syndrome","The most common genetic generalized epilepsy syndrome, presenting in adolescence with morning myoclonic jerks.",B.Mz,"Generalized polyspike-and-wave, often with a photosensitive response.",'Ask every adolescent with "clumsiness" or morning jerks about JME before labeling episodes non-epileptic.',!1)
B.Nt=s(["Forced/resisted eye closure","Asynchronous limb thrashing, side-to-side head shaking","Waxing-and-waning course; often prolonged (over 2 minutes)","Pelvic thrusting","Preserved awareness during bilateral limb movement is a specific sign"],t.s)
B.WU=new A.cd("Psychogenic Non-Epileptic Seizure (PNES)","Non-epileptic","Episodes resembling seizures caused by psychological factors rather than abnormal cortical electrical activity; confirmed by video-EEG capturing a typical event without an ictal correlate.",B.Nt,"No ictal epileptiform correlate on video-EEG during a typical captured event.","PNES and epilepsy commonly coexist \u2014 a PNES diagnosis does not exclude comorbid epilepsy.",!1)
B.PV=s(["Presyncopal prodrome: lightheadedness, tunnel vision, diaphoresis, nausea","Brief duration of jerking, usually under 30 seconds","Rapid, full recovery without prolonged postictal confusion","Situational triggers: prolonged standing, pain, venipuncture, micturition/defecation"],t.s)
B.Xj=new A.cd("Convulsive syncope","Non-epileptic","Transient loss of consciousness from cerebral hypoperfusion accompanied by brief myoclonic jerks or tonic posturing \u2014 a common seizure mimic.",B.PV,"Generalized slowing then flattening during hypoperfusion, normalizing rapidly afterward \u2014 no epileptiform correlate.","Convulsive syncope is the single most common seizure mimic misdiagnosed as epilepsy in adults.",!1)
B.PC=s(["Onset under 1 year, often triggered by fever or heat","Prolonged, often hemiclonic seizures progressing to multiple seizure types","Developmental regression/plateau typically emerges in the second year","High risk of status epilepticus and SUDEP"],t.s)
B.X1=new A.cd("Dravet syndrome","Rare / genetic syndrome","Severe myoclonic epilepsy of infancy, usually caused by an SCN1A sodium-channel mutation, beginning with prolonged febrile/hemiclonic seizures in the first year of life.",B.PC,"Often normal early, later generalized and multifocal spike-wave activity; photosensitivity is common.","A first prolonged febrile seizure before 12 months, especially hemiclonic or heat-triggered, should prompt SCN1A genetic testing for Dravet syndrome.",!0)
B.NK=s(['Triad: tonic, atonic ("drop") and atypical absence seizures',"Usual onset age 3-5 years","Intellectual disability/developmental regression is common","Often refractory to standard anti-seizure medications"],t.s)
B.Xc=new A.cd("Lennox-Gastaut syndrome","Rare / genetic syndrome","A severe childhood-onset epileptic encephalopathy with multiple seizure types, cognitive impairment, and a characteristic slow spike-wave EEG pattern.",B.NK,"Interictal slow (<2.5 Hz) generalized spike-and-wave; bursts of generalized paroxysmal fast activity during sleep.","Drop attacks plus atypical absences plus a slow spike-wave EEG pattern should raise suspicion for Lennox-Gastaut syndrome rather than typical absence epilepsy.",!0)
B.Oj=s(["Onset typically age 3-7 years","Regression of previously normal language, especially auditory verbal agnosia","Overt seizures occur in only about two-thirds of cases","Behavioral change is common"],t.s)
B.X4=new A.cd("Landau-Kleffner syndrome","Rare / genetic syndrome","Acquired epileptic aphasia \u2014 a rare childhood disorder with progressive loss of language comprehension/expression associated with an epileptiform EEG, with or without overt seizures.",B.Oj,"Near-continuous spike-wave activity during slow-wave sleep, often bitemporal.","Unexplained language regression in a young child warrants a sleep EEG to look for Landau-Kleffner syndrome, even without witnessed seizures.",!0)
B.NL=s(["Onset within the first 3 months, often the first days of life","Frequent tonic spasms, sometimes clustering","Severe developmental impairment","May evolve into West syndrome and later Lennox-Gastaut syndrome"],t.s)
B.WX=new A.cd("Ohtahara syndrome","Rare / genetic syndrome","Early infantile epileptic encephalopathy presenting in the first weeks of life with tonic spasms and a burst-suppression EEG pattern; among the earliest-onset and most severe epilepsy syndromes.",B.NL,"Burst-suppression pattern (bursts of high-voltage spikes alternating with near-flat suppression), present in both wakefulness and sleep.","A burst-suppression EEG in a neonate with tonic spasms should prompt urgent evaluation for a structural or metabolic cause of Ohtahara syndrome.",!0)
B.Nh=s(["Usual onset in childhood","Frequent, drug-resistant focal motor seizures","Progressive unilateral weakness and cognitive decline over months to years","Hemispherotomy/hemispherectomy is the most effective treatment in confirmed cases"],t.s)
B.X6=new A.cd("Rasmussen encephalitis","Rare / genetic syndrome","A rare, progressive inflammatory disease of one cerebral hemisphere causing drug-resistant focal seizures, often epilepsia partialis continua, with progressive hemiparesis and cognitive decline.",B.Nh,"Progressive unihemispheric slowing with multifocal epileptiform discharges over the affected hemisphere.","Progressive unilateral weakness accompanying drug-resistant focal seizures in a child should raise suspicion for Rasmussen encephalitis and prompt MRI for unihemispheric atrophy.",!0)
B.OA=s(["Continuous or near-continuous focal clonic jerking, classically of a hand or face","Awareness usually preserved","Can persist for hours to years despite treatment","Causes include Rasmussen encephalitis, stroke, tumor, and metabolic derangement (e.g. non-ketotic hyperglycemia)"],t.s)
B.Xf=new A.cd("Epilepsia partialis continua","Rare / genetic syndrome","A rare form of focal motor status epilepticus with continuous, rhythmic clonic jerking of a body part, sometimes lasting days to years, usually with preserved awareness.",B.OA,"May show focal epileptiform discharges time-locked to the jerking, but scalp EEG is often unremarkable given the small cortical generator.","A normal scalp EEG does not exclude epilepsia partialis continua \u2014 the diagnosis is primarily clinical.",!0)
B.OI=s(["Clusters of brief (seconds to a minute), stereotyped hypermotor/hyperkinetic seizures during sleep","Onset usually in childhood, often with a family history","Frequently confused with NREM parasomnias (sleepwalking, night terrors)","Preserved awareness is common, distinguishing it from typical parasomnias"],t.s)
B.X0=new A.cd("Autosomal dominant nocturnal frontal lobe epilepsy (ADNFLE)","Rare / genetic syndrome","A genetic (often CHRNA4/CHRNB2 nicotinic receptor gene) focal epilepsy causing brief, clustered hypermotor seizures during sleep, frequently misdiagnosed as a parasomnia.",B.OI,"Scalp EEG is frequently normal or non-localizing given the frontal, often mesial, seizure onset.","A strong family history of stereotyped nocturnal events favors ADNFLE over an isolated parasomnia.",!0)
B.On=s(["Peak onset age 3-6 years","Prominent ictal autonomic symptoms: vomiting, pallor, eye deviation","Seizures can be prolonged (autonomic status epilepticus), even though isolated events are otherwise benign","Usually resolves spontaneously within a few years"],t.s)
B.X9=new A.cd("Panayiotopoulos syndrome","Rare / genetic syndrome","A self-limited childhood focal epilepsy dominated by autonomic ictal features \u2014 most strikingly prominent vomiting \u2014 usually during sleep, with an excellent long-term prognosis.",B.On,"Multifocal, often occipital, spikes; interictal EEG can also be normal.","Recurrent nocturnal vomiting with impaired awareness in a young child is a classic, under-recognized presentation of Panayiotopoulos syndrome, not gastroenteritis.",!0)
